module bindings.csharp.deserializationBinder;

// .NET's BinaryFormatter, SoapFormatter and NetDataContractSerializer
// reconstruct whatever type the stream names, and the documented way to bound
// that set is SerializationBinder: assigning one to the formatter's Binder
// property routes every type resolution through BindToType, where the
// implementation decides which types may load. This is the same hardening
// surface JEP 290's setObjectInputFilter is for Java, and like that filter it
// is installed on the reader rather than passed to the read.
//
// The assignment lowers to a call node whose path is the property being
// written (b.Binder), so a segment match on Binder labels the installation
// wherever it happens; the bound class's own name carries no weight, since
// every project names its binder differently. The segment match also reaches
// a mid-path Binder segment (a call through a binder, such as
// binder.BindToType inside a custom implementation), which reads as binder
// usage rather than as an unrelated name. The check anchors at that call
// with endpoint coverage, which is dominance-based: a binder installed
// before the read covers it, while one installed in a sibling branch or
// after the read does not. A member named Binder on a class with no
// serialization role would neutralise nothing an attacker can reach, because
// the coverage still needs a deserialization sink the write dominates.

binding serializationBinderAssignmentIsSafeDeserialization {
  query pattern callExpr where callee.path ~= "Binder"
  emit check core.SafeDeserialization at call {
    covers endpoint {
      anchor: call
    }
  }
}
